; BUG-018 reproducer (plan 018): an imported generic rule whose
; effective type uses `.within <aliased-rfc9052-name>` must resolve
; the `.within` RHS through the generic definition's own import
; scope, not the consumer's.
;
; Topology (matches the dntls-cose-encrypt shape from the plan):
;
; 1. `wrapper_lib.cddl` imports the RFC 9052 standard CDDL as `cose`
;    and declares a generic rule whose effective type uses
;    `.within cose.COSE_Encrypt0`.
; 2. `consumer.cddl` imports `wrapper_lib.cddl` under a different
;    alias (`encrypt`) and instantiates the imported generic with
;    concrete arguments.  The consumer does NOT import `cose`
;    itself.
;
; The `.within` subtype check must complete without surfacing an
; unresolved `cose.COSE_Encrypt0` diagnostic.  The `cose.COSE_Encrypt0`
; reference is supplied by RFC 9052; this is a compiler/linter
; regression, not a CBOR validation case.
;
; Note: the LHS and RHS shapes differ on purpose.  The point of the
; fixture is to verify that `.within cose.COSE_Encrypt0` *resolves*
; through the generic's definition-site import scope.  A subtype
; failure here proves the `.within` RHS was resolved (it cannot
; fail for the wrong reason when the name is unresolved).

root = encrypt.COSE_Encrypt0<uint, nil>

;# import encrypt.COSE_Encrypt0<headers, payload> from "./support/bug_018_wrapper_lib.cddl" as encrypt

